%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5ECB4528] # Import Data and Analyze
%[text] In this exercise, we will import data from a file, visualize that data, and perform some statistical analysis. 
%[text] Online Documentation
%[text] - [Data Types](https://salcedoe.github.io/MtMdocs/dataProcessing/dataTypes/)
%[text] - [Tidy Data](https://salcedoe.github.io/MtMdocs/dataProcessing/tidyData/)
%[text] - [Tables](https://salcedoe.github.io/MtMdocs/matlabBasics/Tables/)
%[text] - [Plotting](https://salcedoe.github.io/MtMdocs/matlabBasics/PlottingComponents/) \
%[text] ## Learning Objectives
%[text] - Import Spreadsheet data as a Table using a function
%[text] - Apply Group statistics to the rows
%[text] - Visualize Data from the table \
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ## Load Tabular data
%[text] In the previous script, we loaded tabular data from the `'teleultrasound_data.xlsx'` excel file in the `MtMresources/data/unit1` folder. We are going to use the same data in this live script
%[text] #### Hard-code the file path
%[text] If you are going to import data from the same file on a regular basis, you may want to hard-code the location of the file so you don't have to keep selecting it each time you launch the live script. One simple way to get the path is to:
%[text] 1. Navigate to the `MtMresources/data/unit1` folder in the Files window
%[text] 2. Right click on the `teleultrasound_data.xlsx` file
%[text] 3. Select "Copy Path"
%[text] 4. Paste it below, in place of `PASTE_PATH_HERE`, and remove the leading `%` to activate the line \
%[text] ![](text:image:3b7d)
%datapath = PASTE_PATH_HERE
%%
%[text] ### Load table
%[text] Now that we have set the datapath, we can use the function `readtable` to read in the data. 
T = readtable(datapath) %[output:930517f7]
%%
%[text] We'll also need to change the Student column to a categorical array, which we can do using the `categorical` function
T.Student = categorical(T.Student);
%%
%[text] ## Data Visualization: With data comes Figures
%[text] It's critical to visualize the data when you first load it. This gives an overview of what your data is like—things like distribution (how spread out the data is) and range. You can get a lot of information about your data at a glance.
%[text] ### On the term figure
%[text] In a paper, a figure is often the visualization itself, like a line plot or histogram. Or it's a collection of charts, often as a series of panels. In MATLAB, figure means the window into which you add the charts.
%[text] **Try it now.** Enter `figure` into the command window
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] figure
%[text] ```
%[text] - You get a window into which you can add charts.
%[text] - Notice the title of the window is "Figures" and there is a small tab called "Figure 1" \
%[text] If you enter `figure` again in the command window, you'll get a second tab called "Figure 2". It is into these tabs that we will be adding our data visualizations
%%
%[text] ### Visualizations in Live Scripts
%[text] By default, live scripts hides the Figures window and the little Figure tabs. Instead, the visualizations are added directly to the live script. We will start by adding a figure into the live script, then we will pop that figure out and inspect the figure in the Figures window. 
%%
%[text] ### Histograms
%[text] The most straight-forward visualization is a histogram. It's typically the visualization of just one column of data. Let's visualize the Score column in `T`. The function `histogram` plots a histogram, as follows:
figure % create new figure
histogram(T.Score)
%[text] - And you get a visualization of the frequency of each score
%[text] - Each bar contains a binned count of the score frequency. The furthest left bar, ranging from 1.5 - 2.5, contains a count of 1. So, one score fell within that range. And so on.
%[text] - As you can see, the score 4 has the highest frequency and the values range from ~1.5 - 5.5 (since this is a histogram, the bins may cover a wider range than the actual range of the data) \
%%
%[text] ### Glow-up your histogram
%[text] The default figure settings often leave a lot to be desired. Often, the font size needs to be enlarged. And maybe the colors changed. We also should add some axes labels.  Fortunately, the Figures window has a lot of settings that can help you make your figure look more compelling.
%[text] To view the figure in the figures window, click on the "Open figure window" icon:
%[text] ![](text:image:1777)
%[text] - Note: this only appears when you hover over the histogram \
%[text] After clicking, the figure should appear in its very own tab in the Figures Window
%[text] #### Figure Tab
%[text] Under the Figure tab, you can copy or save the figure. And you can add things to the figure. 
%[text] 1. Add a title ('Student Quiz Scores')
%[text] 2. Add a Y-Label ('Frequency')
%[text] 3. Add an X-Label ('Score')
%[text] 4. Turn on Y-grid \
%[text] #### Format Tab
%[text] Under the Format tab are tools to change the appearance of the figure. But, first you need to click on the "Select & plot edit" icon
%[text] ![](text:image:1f36)
%[text] - You can now select different parts of the plot, including the axes and the bars themselves
%[text] - Notice that there are three things that you can click on: The figure, the axes, and the histogram. These are separate objects that have their own properties. Click on More Properties to see the properties of each  \
%[text] Change Properties
%[text] 1. Click on the axes and increase the font size to 18
%[text] 2. Click on the bars and change the color in the FaceColor property \
%%
%[text] ## Grouping Variables
%[text] Often in a dataset you want to evaluate a column of data by group. For example, it doesn't quite make sense to plot all the Score data as one histogram because there are actually two groups we want to evaluate in that column: the in-person vs the virtual scores. Instead, we should index out one group of scores and plot those as a histogram, and then index out the other group, and plot those as another histogram.
%[text] Since our data is tidy, we can use the Site column to group the Score Column. 
%[text] #### Index out Different groups of Scores
%[text] We can using logical indexing to pull out the rows needed to calculate the means. 
%[text] How would you create a logical array of the rows in `T` that contain the in-person scores? Assign to `la`.
% la = T.Site == "___";
%%
%[text] Use that logical array to assign to x the 'in person' scores
% x1 = T.Scores(___)
%%
%[text] Without creating a new logical array, how would you assign the  "virtual" scores to a new variable `x2`?
% x2
%%
%[text] ### Overlay Histograms
%[text] Now that we have our to groups of scores, we can plot them as two different histograms. The trick is turn `hold` on
figure
histogram(x1) % histogram of in person scores

hold on % turn on overlay

histogram(x2) % histogram of virtual scores
%%
%[text] ### Box Plots
%[text] Box plots make using grouping variables much easier. You can just plug-in the grouping variable as one of the inputs. 
%[text] The `boxchart` function creates box plots.  Box plots visualize summary statistics for a set of observations. The boxes in said plots are the interquartile range and the whiskers are the full data range. 
%[text] The first input into `boxchart` is the grouping variable (Site). The second input is the data we want to plot (Score)
figure
x = T.Site; % group by site
y = T.Score; % rating

hb = boxchart(x,y);
ylim([0 5])
%[text] - In a box plot, the scores are grouped by Site along the X-Axis
%[text] - The scores themselves are plotted along the Y-axis
%[text] - We don't have upper whiskers in our plots due to the uneven distributions in our scores and the fact that no score can be higher than 5
%[text] - Also, since these are Likert scores, we don't have a huge range or variability in the data \
%%
%[text] #### Data tips
%[text] To get some more information about our box charts, we can add data tips to the plots
%[text] 1. Hover over the plot until the plotting toolbar appears
%[text] 2. Click on the Datatip tool ![](text:image:5917).
%[text] 3. Hover the mouse pointer over one of the box plots
%[text] 4. When the pointer turns into a precision pointer (like a big plus sign), click on the box plot
%[text] 5. You should now see a datatip on the box plot \
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:204e)
%[text] - More information about the plot is displayed. What does this tell us about our data distribution?
%[text] - Where is the median? \
%[text] Change Datatip appearance
%[text] - Right-click on the datatip and change display to "Window Inside Figure"
%[text] - This changes the datatip to a floating window that can be moved around \
%[text] ![](text:image:7f9d)
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ### Plot handles
%[text] Every plotting function returns an output. You don't need to assign this output to a variable for the plotting to work, but if you do, you get live access to the plotted chart's properties. 
%[text] This output is called a plot handle, and we just assigned one to `hb` when we created our box chart.
hb.BoxFaceColor = [1 0 1]; %[control:colorPicker:043a]{"position":[19,26]}
hb.BoxFaceAlpha = .3;
%[text] - Notice that the plot handle is a `BoxChart` handle
%[text] - Click on "Show all properties" to see the plotted properties of the chart in the command window
%[text] - Notice that these properties are the same properties that you see when you bring up the Property Inspector \
%%
%[text] You try: How would you change the whiskers to magenta? (Hint: it's called WhiskerLineColor)

%%
%[text] ### Parent-Child relationships
%[text] In a box plot, the box plot is added to an axes, which is added to a figure. You can return the properties of an object's parent using the object's handle, as follows:
hb.Parent
%[text] - in this case, we get the axes to which the bar plot was added \
%%
%[text] We can even change the properties of the parent. Here we change the color of the axes to black. 
hb.Parent.Color = 'k'
%%
%[text] **Warning**. There is also a function called `boxplot`. This is included with the Statistics Toolbox. It makes slightly uglier box plots, but there is more statistical control. But, it can be more trouble than it's worth
boxplot(x,y)
%[text] - Click "Explain Error" to see why this fails \
%%
%[text] `boxplot` requires the inputs in the exact opposite order as `boxchart`
figure;
boxplot(y,x)
%[text] - So, not quite as nice as `boxchart` \
%%
%[text] ### Swarm Charts
%[text] Box plots visualize the distribution statistics of a set of data, but sometimes the true nature of the distribution remains hidden in a box plot, especially when the sample size is small.
%[text] This is why it is often best to plot all the data points. For grouped data, we can do this using the `swarmchart` function. Notice we are plotting the exact same data, just as a swarm chart.
figure
hs = swarmchart(x,y) % same x and y as before
%[text] - Now we have one dot for each student
%[text] - But, again, the default plot settings leave much to be desired \
%%
%[text] ### Update Marker Properties
%[text] Since we assigned the swarmchart handle to `hs`, we can change the properties of the figure
%[text] Warning: do not close the figure or the handle won't work
hs.MarkerFaceColor = 'k'; % set marker to black
hs.MarkerEdgeColor = 'k'; % edge color (try 'none')
hs.MarkerFaceAlpha = 0.25; % transparency
hs.XJitterWidth = 0.5; % separation between dots (try 0.5 or .15)
%[text] - So, now we see why our box plots look so weird
%[text] - The scores are whole numbers and there is not a lot of variation between student responses \
%%
%[text] ### Overlaying Plots
%[text] It is often desirable to overlay multiple plots in the same axes. In MATLAB, overlay is called `hold` and you can turn hold on or off in a figure.
%[text] For example, let's overlay the swarm charts and the box plots
figure % create figure
hb = boxchart(x,y); % box plot
ylim([0 5]) % adjust the y limits

hold on % TURN ON OVERLAY

hs = swarmchart(x,y,... % swarm chart
    MarkerFaceColor='k',...
    MarkerEdgeColor='k',...
    MarkerFaceAlpha=0.25,...
    XJitterWidth=0.25);
%[text] - Notice in `swarmchart` that we added all of the marker properties settings in the function call. This allows us to set the properties the way we like it \
%[text] Try it now:
%[text] - Turn `hold off`
%[text] - modify the marker colors
%[text] - Resize the docked figure \
%%
%[text] #### Course function 
%[text] The course function `mmBoxSwarm` overlays box plots and swarm charts with one function call
figure;
mmBoxSwarm(x,y)
%%
%[text] You can add inputs to `mmBoxSwarm` to adjust the colors.
%[text] Try it now. 
%[text] After `figure`, type: `mmBoxSwarm(x,y,`
%[text] - Change the Marker Face color to 'k'
%[text] - Change the Box Face Color to red
%[text] - Adjust the `XJitterWidth` to 0.5 \
%[text] Then, change the Y Limits to range from 0 to 5, using `ylim` and add a y-axis label of "Score" using `ylabel`
% figure
% mmBoxSwarm(x,y,'MarkerFaceColor','___','BoxFaceColor','___','XJitterWidth',___)
% ylim(___)
% ylabel(___)
%%
%[text] ### Multiple Groups - Group by Question
%[text] Box plots and Swarm Charts are really useful for displaying multiple grouped data in the same axes. For example, we can plot  the range of scores for each question
%[text] #### Try it now 1
%[text] 1. Set `x` to `Question`
%[text] 2. Set `y` to `Score`
%[text] 3. Plot `x` and `y` using `mmBoxSwarm` \
% x = T.___;
% y = T.___;
% mmBoxSwarm(x,y)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BB9CE18C] ### Using Grouping Variables for stats
%[text] If we want to calculate the stats on our data, we should also use grouping variables. Since we have two categories, in person vs virtual, it makes more sense to calculate two means: one for the in person scores and one for the virtual scores. 
%[text] Consider the following illustration. 
%[text] ![](text:image:943d)
%[text] adapted from [Performing Calculations by Group in Table](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/matlab_prog/perform-calculations-by-group-in-table.html).
%[text] 
%[text] In the Grouping Variable column (colored in Blue and Orange), there are two groups: 'AB' and 'XYZ'. In the Data Variable, there are the data observations you want to measure. Using the grouping variable, we can sort the data in 'AB' or 'XYZ' data, as shown in the "Groups of Data" column. Then we can apply mean to each group of data and compare those means (`82.25` vs `60`). 
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_91CFD3E9] #### Group summary
%[text] The function `groupsummary` does all of the row indexing for you and then applies the statistics.
%[text] Consider the following: 
S = groupsummary(T,"Site",["mean" "std" "median"],"Score")
%[text] Notice
%[text] - With this one line of code, we break our table down into two groups and calculate the mean, standard deviation, and median of the scores.
%[text] - This function returns a new table that organizes the statistical results
%[text] - In the new table, `S`, the in-person scores were higher than the virtual scores.
%[text] - Also notice that the group counts differ between the two groups \
%%
%[text] #### You Try:
%[text] - Calculate the mean and standard deviation score for each student using `groupsummary`.
%[text] - Sort rows of the new table by mean score \
% S_student = groupsummary(T, "___", ["mean" "std"], "___");
% S_student = sortrows(S_student, "___");
%[text] - Which student had the highest score? lowest? \
%%
%[text] ### Bar Plots
%[text] Fortunately, most of the plotting we do does not involve painstaking plotting out point coordinates. Typically, you have a bunch of data that you want to plot out. For example, let's plot the mean of our score as a bar plot.
%[text] #### Step 1. Get data and plot the bars
figure % create a new figure
x = S.Site; % grouping variable
y = S.mean_Score; % mean score

bar(x,y) % create bar plot
ylabel("Score") % y-axis label
title('Tele-Ultrasound Analysis') % title
%[text] - `ylabel` is a shortcut for setting a property of the axes.
%[text] - `title` adds a title \
%%
%[text] #### Step 2. Overlay Error bars
%[text] The function `errorbar` creates error bars. Since this is a separate function, we need to turn the overlay function on, by calling `hold on`. Otherwise, `errorbar` would overwrite the bar plot.
hold on % turn on overlay
e = S.std_Score; % error

errorbar(x,y,e)
%%
%[text] #### Step 3. Clean up Figure Properties
%[text] The default plot settings are usually pretty ugly. Luckily, its easy to prettify a plot. 
%[text] 1. Click on white arrow in Figure tool bar
%[text] 2. Double click on figure and update properties to look more like this: \
%[text] ![](text:image:8685)
%[text] - Increase Font Size
%[text] - Change Bar face color and alpha
%[text] - Change Bar Line Width
%[text] - Change error bar color and thickness
%[text] - Remove connecting line between error bars \
%%
%[text] #### Step 4. Generate Code in Figures Window
%[text] Generate Code and paste here
% paste the generated code here
%%
%[text] ## Multiple Plots in the Same Figure
%[text] The function `nexttile` adds multiple axes to the same figure
figure
nexttile % turn tiling on
%%
%[text] Every time you call `nexttile`, an axes gets added to the figure
nexttile
%[text] - try running this code block multiple times
%[text] - The default layout is called "flow" - this adds the tiles in an ever growing matrix \
%%
%[text] ### Organizing the tiles
%[text] The function `tiledlayout` helps you control the organization of the tiles
figure
tiledlayout("vertical")
%%
nexttile
%[text] - call this block multiple times \
%[text] Now you try:
%[text] Predict: will the axes stack top-to-bottom or sit side-by-side once you switch to "horizontal"?
%[text] 1. Go back and change the input in `tiledlayout` to "horizontal"
%[text] 2. Re-run that code block
%[text] 3. Re-run this codeblock multiple times
%[text] 4. What happens? Was your prediction correct? \
%%
%[text] ### Plotting the Question Statistics
%[text] Let's visualize the Questions statistics both as a Bar Plot and as a Swarm/Box Chart in the same figure, as follows:
%[text] ![](text:image:4c50)
%[text] #### Calculate Mean and Standard Deviation
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_3dc0] For the bar plot, we will plot the mean and standard deviation of each question. To calculate these stats, we use the function `groupsummary`, as follows:
S = groupsummary(T,"Question",["mean" "std"],"Score") % group summary per question
%%
%[text] #### Bar Plots
%[text] First we set up our figure as follows:
figure
tiledlayout("horizontal")
nexttile % new tile for the bar plot
%[text] - these 3 commands create a new figure, set up the tile configuration, and create an empty axis \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:TMP_26f7] Then we add the bar and error plots. These plots will get added to the axis we created in the previous step. 
x = S.Question; % grouping variable
y = S.mean_Score; % mean score
bar(x,y,FaceAlpha=0.5) % create bar plot
hold on

e = S.std_Score; % get the standard deviation as the error
errorbar(x, y, e, 'k', 'LineStyle', 'none'); % add error bars to the bar plot
title("Bar Plot") % add a title to the axis
ylim([0 5.25]); % set the y-axis limits
ylabel('Score'); % Label the y-axis
%[text] - Right now, since there is only 1 tile, the tile automatically fills the figure \
%%
%[text] #### Swarm and Box Overlays
%[text] Next we add the swarm and box plot overlays. But we first need to call `nexttile` to create a new tile. Also, notice for the Swarm and Box Chart, we use the values from the `T` table (because we are plotting every single data point).
nexttile % new tile for Swarm Chart

x = T.Question; % question 
y = T.Score; % rating

mmBoxSwarm(x,y,"MarkerFaceColor",'k')
% Set the y-axis limits and add a y-axis label
ylim([0 5.25]); % set the y-axis limits
title("Swarm + Box Plot overlay") % add a title to the axis
%[text] - Now our tiles are side-by-side
%[text] - What happens if we run this code block again? \
%%
%[text] #### Add Overall Title
%[text] The function `sgtitle` adds a title above all of the tiles
sgtitle("Question Stats") % overall title

